title: Odd or even: parity
mode: vhdl
chapter: Combining gates
check: behaves
---- intro
With three inputs, writing a gate is still one line: operators chain, `a xor b xor c`.

XOR of several bits answers a useful question: is the number of 1s **odd**? Every time a 1 comes in, the answer flips. This is a **parity** bit, and memory chips and network links send it along with the data to spot a bit that went wrong on the way.
---- task
Make `y` 1 when an odd number of `a`, `b` and `c` are 1.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity parity3 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    b : in  std_logic;
    c : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end parity3;

architecture rtl of parity3 is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity parity3 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    b : in  std_logic;
    c : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end parity3;

architecture rtl of parity3 is
begin
    y <= a xor b xor c;
end rtl;
---- hint1
XOR is 1 when its two inputs differ: chain it across all three inputs.
---- hint2
`y <= a xor b xor c;`
---- solution
`y <= a xor b xor c;` One 1 gives 1, two give 0, three give 1 again.
---- done
In a drawing that is two XOR gates in a row; in VHDL, one line. For 32 bits it would still be one line, as you will see with buses.
